.SH PORTABILITY
Applications employing
.I \%ncurses
extensions should condition their use on the visibility of the
.B \%NCURSES_VERSION
preprocessor macro.
X/Open Curses Issue\ 4 describes these functions.
It specifies no error conditions for them.
.PP
SVr4 describes a successful return value only as
\*(``an integer value other than
.IR ERR \*(''. \" Courier roman in source; SVID 4, vol. 3, p. 542
.SS filter
The SVr4 documentation describes the action of
.I \%filter
only in the vaguest terms.
The description here is adapted from X/Open Curses,
which erroneously fails to describe the disabling of
.B \%parm_up_cursor
.RB ( cuu ")."
.SS "\fIdelay_output\fP padding"
The limitation to 30 seconds
and the use of
.I \%napms
differ from other implementations.
.bP
SVr4
.I curses
does not delay if no padding character is available.
.bP
NetBSD
.I curses
uses
.I \%napms
when no padding character is available,
but does not take timing into account when using the padding character.
.PP
Neither limits the delay.
.SS keyname
The
.I \%keyname
function may return the names of user-defined string capabilities
that are defined in the
.I \%terminfo
entry via the \fB\-x\fP
option of \fB@TIC@\fP.
.I \%ncurses
automatically assigns at runtime key codes to
user-defined strings that begin with \*(``k\*(''.
The key codes start at KEY_MAX,
but are not guaranteed to be the same value for different runs
because user-defined codes are merged
from all terminal descriptions that have been loaded.
The \fB\%use_extended_names\fP(3X) function controls
whether this data is loaded
when the terminal description is read by the library.
.SS "\fIputwin\fP/\fIgetwin\fP file format"
The
.I \%putwin
and
.I \%getwin
functions have several issues with portability.
.bP
The files written and read by these functions
use an implementation-specific format.
Although the format is an obvious target for standardization,
it has been overlooked.
.IP
Interestingly enough,
according to the copyright dates in Solaris source,
the functions
(along with
.IR \%scr_init ","
etc.\&)
originated with the University of California,
Berkeley
(in 1982)
and were later
(in 1988)
incorporated into SVr4.
Oddly,
there are no such functions in the 4.3BSD
.I curses
sources.
.bP
Most implementations simply dump the binary
.I \%WINDOW
structure
to the file.
These include SVr4
.IR curses ,
NetBSD
.IR curses ,
and
.IR \%PDCurses ,
as well as older
.I \%ncurses
versions.
.I \%ncurses ","
since version 6 (2015),
and
.I \%xcurses
use textual dumps.
.IP
The implementations that use binary dumps use block I/O
(\fIwrite\fP(2) and \fIread\fP(2) functions).
Those that use textual dumps use buffered I/O.
A few applications may happen to write extra data in the file using
these functions.
Doing that can run into problems mixing block and buffered I/O.
.I \%ncurses
reduces the problem on writes by flushing the output.
However,
reading from a file written using mixed schemes may not be successful.
.SS "unctrl, wunctrl"
X/Open Curses Issue\ 4 describes these functions.
It specifies no error conditions for them.
It states that
.I \%unctrl
and
.I \%wunctrl
return a null pointer if unsuccessful.
.I \%ncurses
checks for three cases.
.bP
The parameter is a 7-bit US-ASCII code.
This is the case that X/Open Curses documented.
.bP
The parameter is in the range 128\-159,
i.e.,
a C1 control code.
If the
.I \%ncurses
extension function \fB\%use_legacy_coding\fP(3X) has been called with a
.B 2
parameter,
.I \%unctrl
returns the parameter,
i.e.,
a one-character string with
the parameter as the first character.
Otherwise,
it returns \*(``\*~@\*('',
\*(``\*~A\*('',
etc.,
analogous to \*(``\*^@\*('',
\*(``\*^A\*('',
C0 controls.
.IP
X/Open Curses does not document whether
.I \%unctrl
can be called before initializing
.IR curses "."
.I \%ncurses
permits that,
and returns the \*(``\*~@\*('',
etc.,
values in that case.
.bP
The parameter value is outside the 0 to 255 range.
.I \%unctrl
returns a null pointer.
.PP
The strings returned by
.I \%unctrl
in
.I \%ncurses
are determined at compile time,
showing C1 controls from the upper-128 codes
with a \*(``\*~\*('' prefix rather than \*(``\*^\*(''.
Other implementations have different conventions.
For example,
they may show both sets of control characters with \*(``\*^\*('',
and strip the parameter to 7 bits.
Or they may ignore C1 controls and treat all of the upper-128 codes as
printable.
.I \%ncurses
uses 8 bits
but does not modify the string to reflect locale.
The
.I \%ncurses
extension function \fB\%use_legacy_coding\fP(3X) allows the caller to
change the output of
.IR \%unctrl "."
.PP
Likewise,
the
.I meta
function allows the caller to change the output of
.IR \%keyname ","
i.e.,
it determines whether to use the \*(``M\-\*('' prefix
for \*(``meta\*('' keys (codes in the range 128 to 255).
Both \fB\%use_legacy_coding\fP(3X) and
.I meta
succeed only after
.I curses
is initialized.
X/Open Curses does not document the treatment of codes 128 to 159.
When treating them as \*(``meta\*('' keys
(or if
.I \%keyname
is called before initializing
.IR curses "),"
.I \%ncurses
returns strings \*(``M\-\*^@\*('',
\*(``M\-\*^A\*('',
etc.
.PP
X/Open Curses documents
.I \%unctrl
as declared in
.IR \%unctrl.h ","
which
.I \%ncurses
does.
However,
.IR \%ncurses 's
.I \%curses.h
includes
.IR \%unctrl.h ","
matching the behavior of SVr4
.IR curses "."
Other implementations may not do that.
.SS "use_env, use_tioctl"
If
.I \%ncurses
is configured to provide the screen pointer functions extension,
you can update the use of the environment
and the operating system's terminal driver
by calling
.I \%use_env
and/or
.I \%use_tioctl
before creating each
.I SCREEN
rather than once only;
see \fBcurs_sp_funcs\fP(3X).
Other implementations of
.I curses
do not provide this feature of
.IR \%use_env "."
